# CSS Learning Notes – Flexbox, Text Styling, Lists, and Inheritance

## 1. CSS `justify-content`

The `justify-content` property is used to arrange and distribute items along the main axis of a flex container.

When `flex-direction: row` is used, the main axis is horizontal. When `flex-direction: column` is used, the main axis is vertical.

### Different values of justify-content

**1. `flex-start`**

Items are placed at the beginning of the container.

```css
.container {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: flex-start;
}
```

**2. `flex-end`**

Items are placed at the end of the container.

```css
.container {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: flex-end;
}
```

**3. `center`**

Items are placed in the center of the container.

```css
.container {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: center;
}
```

**4. `space-between`**

Items are distributed evenly, with the first item at the beginning and the last item at the end. There is no extra space at the outer edges.

```css
.container {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: space-between;
}
```

**5. `space-around`**

Equal space is placed around each item. The space between two items is twice the space at the outer edges.

```css
.container {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: space-around;
}
```

**6. `space-evenly`**

Equal space is placed between every item and the container edges.

```css
.container {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: space-evenly;
}
```

**7. `start` and `end`**

- `start` places items at the beginning of the container's main axis.
- `end` places items at the end of the container's main axis.

**8. `left` and `right`**

These values align items to the left or right side of the container when that direction is supported.

**Important:** `justify-content` distributes items along the main axis. `align-items` controls their alignment along the cross axis.

