## 2. CSS Inheritance

CSS inheritance means that some CSS properties automatically pass from a parent element to its child elements.

For example:

```html
<div class="parent">
    <h2>Hello World</h2>
    <p>Learning CSS inheritance.</p>
</div>
```

```css
.parent {
    color: blue;
    font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
}
```

Here, the heading and paragraph inherit the text color and font family from their parent element.

### The `inherit` keyword

We can also use the `inherit` keyword to tell an element to take a property's value from its parent.

```css
.child {
    color: inherit;
}
```

### Common inherited properties

- `color`
- `font-family`
- `font-size`
- `font-style`
- `text-align`
- `line-height`

### Common non-inherited properties

- `margin`
- `padding`
- `border`
- `width`
- `height`
- `background-color`

**Remember:** Not every CSS property is inherited automatically. The `inherit` keyword can explicitly make a child use its parent's computed value.

