## 3. CSS List Styling

CSS provides different properties to control the appearance and position of list items.

### `list-style-type`

This property changes the marker or bullet style of a list.

```css
ul {
    list-style-type: none;
}
```

Some other values include:

- `disc` – filled circle
- `circle` – hollow circle
- `square` – square marker
- `decimal` – numbers
- `lower-alpha` – lowercase letters

### `list-style-position`

This property controls where the list marker appears in relation to the list item's text.

```css
ul {
    list-style-position: inside;
}
```

Two common values:

- `inside` – the marker is placed inside the list item's content area.
- `outside` – the marker is placed outside the list item's content area (default).

### `text-indent`

This property controls the indentation of the first line of text in an element.

```css
p {
    text-indent: 50px;
}
```

The first line of each paragraph starts 50 pixels from its normal starting position.

### `text-transform`

This property changes the capitalization of text.

```css
h2 {
    text-transform: capitalize;
}
```

Common values:

- `uppercase` – converts text to uppercase.
- `lowercase` – converts text to lowercase.
- `capitalize` – makes the first letter of each word uppercase.
- `none` – keeps the original text casing.

---

## 4. CSS Text Alignment

The `text-align` property controls the horizontal alignment of inline content inside a block element.

```css
p {
    text-align: center;
}
```

Common values:

- `left` – aligns text to the left.
- `right` – aligns text to the right.
- `center` – places text in the center.
- `justify` – spreads text so that both edges are aligned where possible.
- `start` – aligns text to the start of the writing direction.
- `end` – aligns text to the end of the writing direction.

Example:

```css
h1 {
    text-align: center;
}

p {
    text-align: justify;
}
```

---

## 5. CSS Font Family

The `font-family` property defines which font should be used to display text.

```css
body {
    font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
}
```

Some commonly used font families include:

- Arial
- Times New Roman
- Georgia
- Verdana
- Courier New

We can also provide multiple fonts as fallbacks.

```css
p {
    font-family: "Times New Roman", Georgia, serif;
}
```

The browser tries the first font. If it is unavailable, it tries the next one.

